  "question": "Is simple personally acquired successful-port behavior representable with full copying/division under the nine-slot limit, independently of cross-location navigation?",
  "symbolic_control_witness": "Nine slots; entry0: FORAGE(port register),1: READ(feedback register),2: IF feedback register zero go7 else fallthrough3,3:COPY,4:DIVIDE,5:JUMP0,6:unreachable padding,7:SET feedback register to1,8:XOR port register with feedback register then wrap0. Two distinct existing registers. This is a symbolic static feasibility witness, not a serialized genome, inserted founder, selected seed or executed program.",
  "control_argument": "Blank port0 probes first. Success feedback1 passes to COPY/DIVIDE then loops without changing the port. Failure feedback0 goes through paid SET1 and XOR to flip the port, then wraps. COPY executes once per successful feeding cycle; exactly nine successful COPY actions fill27 nursery words. Earlier DIVIDE attempts refuse and still pay their base fee. Ninth DIVIDE succeeds if resources and energy guards hold. No nursery-completion opcode is needed for this particular periodic loop.",
  "newborn_behavior": "The same code can restart from blank registers/pc0 at its parent site and reacquire its own port; no private cache is inherited. Code length27 and unchanged opcode/operand ranges permit the symbolic layout, but literal ancestor reachability is not demonstrated.",
